{"built":"2026-11-26T00:18:22.294Z","examples":[{"id":"o1","section":"Ordering up","title":"Right bower and an ace, first to speak","subtitle":"You're left of the dealer, so ordering up hands the opponents the turned-up card.","question":"Order it up, or pass?","sit":{"kind":"bid","dealer":3,"upcard":3,"hand":2134052,"events":[],"scoreUs":0,"scoreThem":0},"scores":[[0,0]],"note":"Pass. With realistic play, ordering up gets you euchred about half the time (49%): you hold only two trumps, and ordering hands the dealer a third. Passing keeps the right bower for defence, and your side still scores 41% of the time — mostly by euchring whoever names trump.\n\nThe gap is 0.18 points per hand (±0.04), about 1.3 points of game-win chance. The see-every-card method calls this a coin flip; it flatters players who order into the dealer. Across 1,440 random hands, makers who ordered up into an opposing dealer made it 87% of the time with every card visible but only 78% with realistic play.","results":[{"label":"","hand":2134052,"realistic":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.18458,0.22458,0.00042,0.31708,0.27333,0],"ev":-0.2283,"se":0.0319,"wins":[0.4951],"pairSe":{"1":0.0425,"5":0.0319}},{"key":1,"bins":[0.46083,0.05167,0,0,0.4875,0],"ev":-0.4108,"se":0.0319,"wins":[0.48195],"pairSe":{"0":0.0425,"5":0.0319}}],"perfect":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.20885,0.33975,0.00005,0.2309,0.22045,0],"ev":0.2167,"se":0.0115,"wins":[0.52573]},{"key":1,"bins":[0.55255,0.14985,0,0,0.2976,0],"ev":0.2571,"se":0.0107,"wins":[0.52801]}],"realisticDeals":2400,"perfectDeals":20000}]},{"id":"o2","section":"Ordering up","title":"Partner turned up the right bower","subtitle":"You hold the left bower and one small trump.","question":"Order your partner up, or pass?","sit":{"kind":"bid","dealer":2,"upcard":20,"hand":2154512,"events":[{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0}],"scoreUs":0,"scoreThem":0},"scores":[[0,0]],"note":"A slight edge to passing (0.07 points ± 0.03), even though ordering makes it 93% of the time. Passing doesn't throw away the right bower: your partner, the dealer, can still pick it up. And if diamonds get turned down, you can name hearts in round 2 holding the J♥ as the right bower with the Q♥ and 10♥. That's why passing leads to more sweeps (35% vs 28%).\n\nBoth methods agree this is close. Either choice is reasonable; passing keeps more options open.","results":[{"label":"","hand":2154512,"realistic":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.50776,0.26781,0.08035,0.09347,0.03881,0.01179],"ev":1.1465,"se":0.0289,"wins":[0.56809],"pairSe":{"4":0.028,"8":0.0289}},{"key":4,"bins":[0.648,0.28306,0,0,0.06894,0],"ev":1.0762,"se":0.0194,"wins":[0.56296],"pairSe":{"0":0.028,"8":0.0194}}],"perfect":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.48978,0.29329,0.0658,0.09236,0.04851,0.01026],"ev":1.1092,"se":0.0099,"wins":[0.56552]},{"key":4,"bins":[0.59576,0.32869,0,0,0.07555,0],"ev":1.102,"se":0.0071,"wins":[0.5648]}],"realisticDeals":2400,"perfectDeals":20000}]},{"id":"o3","section":"Ordering up","title":"Both bowers, but the dealer is an opponent","subtitle":"You're the last player before the dealer.","question":"Order it up, or pass and hope for round 2?","sit":{"kind":"bid","dealer":1,"upcard":18,"hand":1228801,"events":[{"t":"bid","seat":2,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0}],"scoreUs":0,"scoreThem":0},"scores":[[0,0]],"note":"Pass, by 0.13 points (±0.04). Ordering up gives you diamonds with only two trumps (both bowers) and hands the dealer the 9♦. If you pass and diamonds are turned down, you can name hearts in round 2, where the J♥ and J♦ become the right and left bowers alongside the A♥ and Q♥ — four trumps. Passing leads to a sweep 41% of the time.\n\nThe see-every-card method leans slightly towards ordering up. Realistic play doesn't.","results":[{"label":"","hand":1228801,"realistic":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.21973,0.39823,0.01532,0.22626,0.1379,0.00255],"ev":0.5652,"se":0.0344,"wins":[0.54974],"pairSe":{"4":0.0417,"8":0.0344}},{"key":4,"bins":[0.64802,0.12206,0,0,0.22992,0],"ev":0.4323,"se":0.0294,"wins":[0.54005],"pairSe":{"0":0.0417,"8":0.0294}}],"perfect":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.19174,0.50278,0.01266,0.17522,0.1153,0.00229],"ev":0.8329,"se":0.0115,"wins":[0.5682]},{"key":4,"bins":[0.55376,0.31157,0,0,0.13467,0],"ev":0.9075,"se":0.0092,"wins":[0.57294]}],"realisticDeals":2400,"perfectDeals":20000}]},{"id":"d1","section":"Dealer: pick it up or pass?","title":"Left bower, an ace, and the 10 to pick up","subtitle":"Everyone has passed to you.","question":"Pick it up, or turn it down?","sit":{"kind":"bid","dealer":0,"upcard":13,"hand":1179984,"events":[{"t":"bid","seat":1,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":2,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0}],"scoreUs":0,"scoreThem":0},"scores":[[0,0]],"note":"Pick it up, by 0.23 points (±0.04) — about 1.5 points of game-win. With the 10♥ you'll hold three trumps (the J♦ as left bower, the A♥ and the 10♥). Picking up makes it 81% of the time.\n\nThe see-every-card method calls it nearly even. With realistic play the dealer does better than the shortcut suggests: across random hands, dealers who picked up made it 75% of the time with realistic play vs 70% with every card visible.","results":[{"label":"","hand":1179984,"realistic":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.09029,0.36626,0.00126,0.51309,0.0291,0],"ev":0.2566,"se":0.034,"wins":[0.50733],"pairSe":{"3":0.0419,"7":0.0452}},{"key":3,"bins":[0.73714,0.0694,0,0,0.19345,0],"ev":0.489,"se":0.029,"wins":[0.5223],"pairSe":{"0":0.0419,"7":0.0355}},{"key":7,"bins":[0.57356,0,0.00276,0,0.42368,0],"ev":-0.2628,"se":0.0349,"wins":[0.47056],"pairSe":{"0":0.0452,"3":0.0355}}],"perfect":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.08683,0.29135,0.00169,0.58041,0.03972,0],"ev":0.0165,"se":0.0115,"wins":[0.4908]},{"key":3,"bins":[0.58681,0.10028,0,0,0.31291,0],"ev":0.1615,"se":0.012,"wins":[0.49985]},{"key":7,"bins":[0.37689,0,0.01465,0,0.60846,0],"ev":-0.7814,"se":0.0126,"wins":[0.43497]}],"realisticDeals":2400,"perfectDeals":20000}]},{"id":"d2","section":"Dealer: pick it up or pass?","title":"Two aces and two small trumps","subtitle":"Everyone has passed to you.","question":"Pick it up, or turn it down?","sit":{"kind":"bid","dealer":0,"upcard":21,"hand":400384,"events":[{"t":"bid","seat":1,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":2,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0}],"scoreUs":0,"scoreThem":0},"scores":[[0,0]],"note":"Too close to call: passing is ahead by just 0.06 points with a margin of ±0.05. Picking up gives you two small trumps and three good off-suit cards, but you're euchred 39% of the time. Passing lets you defend with two aces.\n\nThis is a judgement call. Against cautious opponents who rarely name a weak trump in round 2, picking up looks better; against aggressive ones, passing does.","results":[{"label":"","hand":400384,"realistic":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.08088,0.31223,0.00275,0.49027,0.08513,0.02873],"ev":-0.0591,"se":0.0387,"wins":[0.4856],"pairSe":{"4":0.0488,"8":0.0387}},{"key":4,"bins":[0.568,0.04393,0,0,0.38807,0],"ev":-0.1203,"se":0.0358,"wins":[0.48041],"pairSe":{"0":0.0488,"8":0.0358}}],"perfect":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.07693,0.24903,0.00182,0.47324,0.16759,0.03139],"ev":-0.3517,"se":0.0134,"wins":[0.4654]},{"key":4,"bins":[0.50505,0.05949,0,0,0.43546,0],"ev":-0.2469,"se":0.0128,"wins":[0.47174]}],"realisticDeals":2400,"perfectDeals":20000}]},{"id":"r1","section":"Round 2: naming trump","title":"Calling \"next\" with one trump","subtitle":"Diamonds were turned down and you speak first. Hearts (same colour) is 'next'.","question":"Call hearts, or pass?","sit":{"kind":"bid","dealer":3,"upcard":19,"hand":8423504,"events":[{"t":"bid","seat":0,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":1,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":2,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0}],"scoreUs":0,"scoreThem":0},"scores":[[0,0]],"note":"Pass. 'Calling next' (naming the suit of the same colour as the one turned down) with a single trump gets you euchred 54% of the time with realistic play. The aces look good, but one queen of trump isn't enough. Passing is better by 0.22 points (±0.06).\n\nThe see-every-card method calls it a tie, because it assumes you'll always play your aces at exactly the right moment.","results":[{"label":"","hand":8423504,"realistic":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.04452,0.24135,0.00234,0.56594,0.13456,0.01128],"ev":-0.3436,"se":0.0495,"wins":[0.48751],"pairSe":{"1":0.057,"2":0.0623,"3":0.0633,"5":0.0495,"6":0.0495,"7":0.0495}},{"key":1,"bins":[0.30946,0.02867,0,0,0.66187,0],"ev":-0.9569,"se":0.0488,"wins":[0.44434],"pairSe":{"0":0.057,"2":0.06,"3":0.0727,"5":0.0488,"6":0.0488,"7":0.0488}},{"key":2,"bins":[0.39437,0.02263,0,0,0.583,0],"ev":-0.7264,"se":0.0503,"wins":[0.4602],"pairSe":{"0":0.0623,"1":0.06,"3":0.073,"5":0.0503,"6":0.0503,"7":0.0503}},{"key":3,"bins":[0.41184,0.04957,0,0,0.53859,0],"ev":-0.5662,"se":0.052,"wins":[0.47125],"pairSe":{"0":0.0633,"1":0.0727,"2":0.073,"5":0.052,"6":0.052,"7":0.052}}],"perfect":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.0418,0.29579,0.00021,0.55677,0.09487,0.01056],"ev":-0.1545,"se":0.0175,"wins":[0.50057]},{"key":1,"bins":[0.33693,0.06436,0,0,0.5987,0],"ev":-0.7318,"se":0.018,"wins":[0.45987]},{"key":2,"bins":[0.40577,0.06652,0,0,0.52771,0],"ev":-0.5166,"se":0.0182,"wins":[0.47468]},{"key":3,"bins":[0.40596,0.15116,0,0,0.44288,0],"ev":-0.1775,"se":0.0191,"wins":[0.4981]}],"realisticDeals":2400,"perfectDeals":20000}]},{"id":"r2","section":"Round 2: naming trump","title":"Two small clubs or two big hearts?","subtitle":"Spades were turned down; the player before you passed.","question":"Which suit — or pass?","sit":{"kind":"bid","dealer":2,"upcard":9,"hand":8585219,"events":[{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":0,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":1,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":2,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0}],"scoreUs":0,"scoreThem":0},"scores":[[0,0]],"note":"Genuinely too close to call. Clubs (next: two small trumps, plus the chance your partner holds the J♠ as left bower), hearts (K♥ A♥) and passing are all within 0.14 points of each other, and every gap is inside its margin of error (about ±0.10).\n\nThe one clear lesson: don't name diamonds — it's 0.64 points worse than clubs.","results":[{"label":"","hand":8585219,"realistic":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.0882,0.34427,0.00597,0.47631,0.07895,0.0063],"ev":0.1412,"se":0.0778,"wins":[0.49936],"pairSe":{"1":0.0898,"3":0.0886,"4":0.0885,"5":0.0778,"7":0.0778,"8":0.0778}},{"key":1,"bins":[0.6245,0.10267,0,0,0.27283,0],"ev":0.2842,"se":0.0713,"wins":[0.50828],"pairSe":{"0":0.0898,"3":0.104,"4":0.108,"5":0.0713,"7":0.0713,"8":0.0713}},{"key":3,"bins":[0.6104,0.08328,0,0,0.30632,0],"ev":0.1643,"se":0.073,"wins":[0.50002],"pairSe":{"0":0.0886,"1":0.104,"4":0.0919,"5":0.073,"7":0.073,"8":0.073}},{"key":4,"bins":[0.48342,0.04923,0,0,0.46735,0],"ev":-0.3528,"se":0.0777,"wins":[0.46445],"pairSe":{"0":0.0885,"1":0.108,"3":0.0919,"5":0.0777,"7":0.0777,"8":0.0777}}],"perfect":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.10308,0.24429,0.00187,0.4694,0.17433,0.00704],"ev":-0.2471,"se":0.0257,"wins":[0.47252]},{"key":1,"bins":[0.5434,0.09337,0,0,0.36323,0],"ev":0.0037,"se":0.0259,"wins":[0.489]},{"key":3,"bins":[0.55117,0.07965,0,0,0.36918,0],"ev":-0.0279,"se":0.0258,"wins":[0.48681]},{"key":4,"bins":[0.35842,0.05632,0,0,0.58526,0],"ev":-0.6995,"se":0.0263,"wins":[0.44065]}],"realisticDeals":2400,"perfectDeals":20000}]},{"id":"r3","section":"Round 2: naming trump","title":"Stuck as dealer with a poor hand","subtitle":"Hearts were turned down and everyone passed again. Stick the dealer: you must name a suit.","question":"Which suit?","sit":{"kind":"bid","dealer":0,"upcard":13,"hand":1082120,"events":[{"t":"bid","seat":1,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":2,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":0,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":1,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":2,"a":0},{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0}],"scoreUs":0,"scoreThem":0},"scores":[[0,0]],"note":"Diamonds, narrowly. Holding just the J♦ as right bower, diamonds edges spades (right bower plus queen) by 0.22 points, but the margin is ±0.12, so treat those two as close. Clubs is clearly worst, 0.47 points behind diamonds.\n\nWhen you're stuck with a bad hand, you're euchred whichever suit you pick (48% even for diamonds). The aim is losing less often.","results":[{"label":"","hand":1082120,"realistic":[{"key":1,"bins":[0.36069,0.00587,0,0,0.63344,0],"ev":-0.8945,"se":0.0871,"wins":[0.42718],"pairSe":{"2":0.103,"4":0.108,"5":0.0871,"6":0.0871,"8":0.0871}},{"key":2,"bins":[0.43909,0.00922,0,0,0.5517,0],"ev":-0.6459,"se":0.0901,"wins":[0.44426],"pairSe":{"1":0.103,"4":0.1165,"5":0.0901,"6":0.0901,"8":0.0901}},{"key":4,"bins":[0.50556,0.01555,0,0,0.47889,0],"ev":-0.4211,"se":0.0909,"wins":[0.4597],"pairSe":{"1":0.108,"2":0.1165,"5":0.0909,"6":0.0909,"8":0.0909}}],"perfect":[{"key":1,"bins":[0.23259,0.00869,0,0,0.75872,0],"ev":-1.2675,"se":0.0273,"wins":[0.40156]},{"key":2,"bins":[0.32292,0.01404,0,0,0.66304,0],"ev":-0.9751,"se":0.0302,"wins":[0.42165]},{"key":4,"bins":[0.37225,0.01388,0,0,0.61387,0],"ev":-0.8277,"se":0.031,"wins":[0.43177]}],"realisticDeals":2400,"perfectDeals":20000}]},{"id":"a1","section":"Going alone","title":"Partner deals the ace — alone or not?","subtitle":"Under your house rule, if you go alone your partner still picks up the ace but sits out with it.","question":"Order up with your partner, or go alone?","sit":{"kind":"bid","dealer":2,"upcard":23,"hand":1590528,"events":[{"t":"bid","seat":3,"a":0}],"scoreUs":0,"scoreThem":0},"scores":[[0,0]],"note":"Go alone, by 0.20 points (±0.03), even though your partner's ace is wasted. Under your house rule, the dealer still picks up the A♦ but sits out with it. Your three trumps (both bowers and the 10♦) take all five tricks 30% of the time for 4 points, and you're euchred only 4% of the time.\n\nThe see-every-card method calls this a tie. Realistic defenders can't see where your partner's strength is, which helps a loner.","results":[{"label":"","hand":1590528,"realistic":[{"key":0,"bins":[0.188,0.5351,0.01108,0.20825,0.04916,0.00841],"ev":0.9623,"se":0.0298,"wins":[0.55578],"pairSe":{"4":0.029,"8":0.0412}},{"key":4,"bins":[0.43395,0.5648,0,0,0.00125,0],"ev":1.5611,"se":0.0105,"wins":[0.59668],"pairSe":{"0":0.029,"8":0.0307}},{"key":8,"bins":[0.66342,0,0.2959